#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184031 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22d992a1-2ea8-5226-9971-188447f98ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Higher-Degree Taylor Polynomial Of a Given Function About a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "05dec044-4b3b-5f42-88c5-b7ab323b0e97" :question/math-academy-id "e-743" :question/problem "Let $f(x) = \\sin{x}.$ Calculate the fourth-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nP_4(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\dfrac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 + \\dfrac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3 + \\dfrac{f^{(4)}(a)}{4!}(x-a)^4.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right),$ $f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right),$ $f''\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right),$ $f'''\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ and $f^{(4)}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P_4(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =1-\\frac{1}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a832b1a2-e80e-53d0-bb64-03df0f1a209c" :knowledge-point/title "Obtaining a Higher-Order Derivative Of a Function Given its Higher-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "38bd1eff-19ac-5922-aa68-f9c30c9f5ea3" :question/math-academy-id "e-744" :question/problem "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nP_4(x) = 1+2x-3x^2+4x^3-5x^4.\n$$\n\nWhat is $f^{(4)}(0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The coefficient of $x^4$ is given by $\\dfrac{f^{(4)}(0)}{4!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(0)}{4!} & =-5 \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(4)}(0)}{24} & =-5 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(0) & =-120.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
